From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Stedding Authors <noreply@stedding.dev>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 12:39:41 -0400
Subject: add the Space model
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A Space is a named set of tabs the user switches between, with its own pinned
tabs, groups, folders and splits. This is the data layer: which Spaces a window
has and which one is active. Moving tabs between them comes next.

A Space is active (its tabs live in the window's TabStripModel) or inactive (it
owns a parked TabStripCollection). Switching will move the tree with the
existing detach-and-insert APIs so pinned state, groups, folders and splits
survive rather than being flattened and rebuilt.

Deliberately not a window and not a tab group. A window would multiply windows
and split the session model; a group would inherit the contiguity and
one-group-per-tab assumptions that groups carry throughout the codebase, which
is the same trap that made Folders a separate collection type.

It attaches through EmbedderBrowserWindowFeatures, which upstream provides for
exactly this — its own comment says it is for Chromium embedders and its methods
are empty stubs reading "Embedder implementation goes here". That file sees two
commits a year, so this is close to the cheapest attachment point available.

SpaceId has no default constructor, so the default Space's id is generated first
and used for both the Space and the active pointer. That is deliberate: an id
naming nothing is not a state worth representing.

Removing the active Space or the last Space is refused rather than half-handled,
because a window must always have somewhere for its tabs to be.

Nine unit tests cover construction, add, lookup, activation, both refusals,
rename, recolour, and that re-activating the current Space does not notify.

Why: Spaces are the feature people stay in Arc for, and the workflow layer the
sidebar exists to serve.
Removable when: never, short of abandoning the product thesis. If upstream ships
an equivalent multi-tab-set concept, this rebases onto it.
